00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.620
Now back to the optimizers that update the&nbsp;
weights of a neural network during training.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.920
The optimizer determines a new, hopefully better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:40.380
weight for a neural network by adding&nbsp;
or subtracting a certain value from it.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:45.300
The SGD optimizer determines&nbsp;
this value to be a fraction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:49.980
the gradients of the loss with respect to&nbsp;
the weight. This basically means that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:54.960
should go a step into the direction of&nbsp;
steepest descent to minimize the loss.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.780
One very important decision that the optimizer&nbsp;
takes, is to determine how far to move into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:07.440
direction that minimizes the loss. If it goes too&nbsp;
far, it can overshoot a minimum. If it goes too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.960
little, the neural network training will take&nbsp;
forever. Here, by using this step size as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:19.020
example, we basically explained the learning rate&nbsp;
hyperparameter of SGD. Other optimizers do even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:23.400
more complicated things than that, for example&nbsp;
they use momentum, in which the weight update&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:29.040
of the current step uses some component of the&nbsp;
update direction from the previous update step.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.060
But it is not the topic of today’s&nbsp;
video to give an overview about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.020
entire zoo of optimizers. But&nbsp;
keep in mind that there are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:41.400
lot of them and there are a lot of&nbsp;
strategies to do the weight updates.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.620
Even worse, each of these little hacks&nbsp;
come with a hyperparameter that decides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.940
how much of this little hack should act&nbsp;
at a certain time step during training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:55.500
for example the learning rate can vary&nbsp;
during the training run and you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:02.160
decide upon a learning rate schedule. So, it&nbsp;
is hard to manually decide what is the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.680
strategy to update the weights for each neural&nbsp;
architecture and for each different dataset.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.540
Therefore, to see how well a set of&nbsp;
hyperparameters do, one needs to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:19.140
one whole training run of a network, see how low&nbsp;
the loss gets. Then change the hyperparameters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:23.700
and do yet another run. And then another&nbsp;
configuration, and another and so on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:29.400
so this becomes really expensive really&nbsp;
fast, until finally, after tens or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:35.460
hundreds of runs, we can take the set of&nbsp;
hyperparameters that we saw performed best.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:38.640
But don’t worry, because this paper proposes VeLO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.080
which is a learned optimizer that can&nbsp;
do the important decisions on its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:49.560
and does not have any hyperparameters! The&nbsp;
idea of this paper is to do meta-learning:

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.860
we can use training runs of diverse architectures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:56.820
and datasets to teach a neural&nbsp;
network to act as an optimizer.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:02.700
The network would take weight and gradient&nbsp;
information and predict how to update the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:08.340
weights to minimize the loss of the target&nbsp;
neural network. Of course, this idea is not new,

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:14.040
learned optimizers have been here for around&nbsp;
a while, but no one has trained optimizers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:19.680
at this scale before and for this range of&nbsp;
tasks, going towards a more general-purpose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.660
optimizer that can be used out-of-the-box&nbsp;
for many datasets and applications.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:29.820
So how does it work? What do&nbsp;
the authors need to do to train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:35.100
the VeLO neural network to act as an&nbsp;
optimizer for other neural networks?

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:40.620
First, they need to assemble the training dataset&nbsp;
for VeLO consisting of models of different model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:46.860
families such as multi-layer-perceptrons,&nbsp;
convolutional networks, residual networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:51.780
transformers, recurrent neural networks,&nbsp;
auto-encoders, variational autoencoders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.600
These all have different depths,&nbsp;
layer widths and activation functions.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.100
To augment the data, the authors even&nbsp;
do things like reparameterizing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.620
weights or changing the floating-point precision.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:11.280
These models are to be trained on tasks ranging&nbsp;
from image classification to image generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:16.980
text generation and training learned&nbsp;
optimizers. Keep in mind the variety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:21.900
of models and tasks captured by this&nbsp;
training data for VeLO, because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:27.060
variety in the training data is highly likely&nbsp;
to determine its capabilities at test time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:32.940
because models usually perform well on the type&nbsp;
of settings they have already seen in training and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:39.240
fail to generalize on tasks out of distribution&nbsp;
(no spoilers, more in this later in the video).

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:45.720
VeLO acts like this: For every single weight, so&nbsp;
for each scalar in the target neural network which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:52.680
it aims to optimize, at each timestep, it predicts&nbsp;
how much to subtract from the weight to update it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:57.240
We say here subtract, but of course that&nbsp;
if it decides to subtract a negative value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.940
it is like it would have&nbsp;
added something to the weight.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:04.860
That VeLO applies to each single&nbsp;
weight of the neural net it optimizes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:09.780
comes with the advantage that in this way,&nbsp;
it can be applied to any neural network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:15.840
independently of its architecture and number&nbsp;
of parameters. But the downside is that it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:20.400
become really costly to apply VeLO as the&nbsp;
number of weights increases in a network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:24.420
therefore the authors strive&nbsp;
to keep VeLO really small.

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:29.580
Therefore, the lightweight architecture of&nbsp;
VeLO is composed of a per-tensor network and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:36.240
a per-weight network as follows: The per-tensor&nbsp;
network is an LSTM which takes information about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:42.240
a parameter tensor. In this way, the authors&nbsp;
make sure that when VeLO predicts an update&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.920
value for each parameter, the update is&nbsp;
informed on other weights of the network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:52.920
As input the authors use, we cite “the&nbsp;
mean and variance of parameter values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:59.160
the exponential moving averages of the gradient&nbsp;
and squared gradient (as used in the Adam update).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:05.160
The per-tensor network also takes as input a&nbsp;
series of additional features representing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:10.620
current fraction of training completed, so that&nbsp;
it can learn training-time dependent strategies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:16.140
such as learning rate schedules. Finally, our&nbsp;
per-tensor network has access to the training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:20.940
loss which can enable complex behaviours&nbsp;
such as detecting divergence of the loss.”

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:26.040
Then the LSTM predicts the weights of a&nbsp;
subsequent network, the per-weight network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:32.640
which is multi-layer-perceptron or MLP in&nbsp;
short. This MLP is also extremely small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:39.360
of just 2 hidden layers with 4 hidden units. This&nbsp;
is the one that uses per-weight information such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:45.660
as gradients and the value of the weight as input&nbsp;
to predict the update for that weight. Previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:51.540
work from the same authors did this differently,&nbsp;
and the MLP took weight information and also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:57.960
hidden representation delivered by the LSTM and&nbsp;
learned its own weights. But this made the MLP’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:03.840
input larger, therefore slower. So, the authors&nbsp;
decide for the LSTM to train to deliver the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:09.780
weight of the MLP directly. And we remind you&nbsp;
how important it is to make VeLO lightweight,

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:14.700
since it must do an inference step for each&nbsp;
weight of a neural network during training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:21.120
so it’s speed directly affects the training speed&nbsp;
of the target neural network we aim to optimize.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:27.000
The authors train VeLO to minimize the training&nbsp;
loss at the end of the training of the target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:33.300
neural network. Yes, and by this we mean that&nbsp;
an entire training run of the target network is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:39.540
one data point for VeLO. So, getting VeLO to&nbsp;
train and to converge is not a cheap task at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:48.480
all. The authors need roughly 4 weeks on 1,000 to&nbsp;
5,000 TPUs. Imagine that. But remember that VeLO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:54.360
once trained, has no hyperparameter to set because&nbsp;
the whole idea of this learned optimizer is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.360 --> 00:10:00.780
figure out by itself what the right strategy for&nbsp;
each training step, model and task is. So, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:06.600
authors argue that VeLO’s training cost is minimal&nbsp;
compared to the amount of compute one saves for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:12.420
not having to train models multiple times to&nbsp;
find the right hyperparameters for optimizers.

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:18.660
So how well does VeLO work in practice? The&nbsp;
already existing benchmark the authors could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:25.740
use to test VeLO on, was the MLCommons workloads&nbsp;
where on 5 out of 6 tasks, VeLO performed better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:32.220
than the Adam optimizer which had a hand-tuned&nbsp;
learning rate schedule and weight decay. But to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:36.300
have a more extensive benchmark that&nbsp;
the authors and future work can use,

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:43.560
this paper also proposes the VeLOdrome&nbsp;
benchmark that encompasses 83 tasks. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:48.960
idea of these tasks is to be cheap in terms&nbsp;
of training time, so one can test quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:54.960
thus the 83 tasks do not need longer&nbsp;
than an hour to train on one GPU.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:11:00.360
The other thing is that these tasks should&nbsp;
cover a wide range of architectures, such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:05.880
convolutional networks, autoencoders, ResNets,&nbsp;
recurrent neural networks and transformers.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:11.640
The good news is, that VeLO performs really&nbsp;
well on these tasks, even when compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:18.060
powerful optimizers such as NAdamW that&nbsp;
had hand-tuned hyperparameters from more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:22.980
than 1000 training runs. So VeLO, that&nbsp;
does not have any hyperparamters could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:30.180
outrun NAdamW out-of-the-box and get better&nbsp;
performance in 85% of the cases. And it got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:35.940
there faster than Adam. On half the VeLOdrome&nbsp;
tasks it was even 4 times faster than Adam.

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:39.660
But these good results on&nbsp;
VeLOdrome are a bit expected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:44.940
since the VeLOdrome benchmark contains exactly&nbsp;
the kind of settings that VeLO was trained on:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:49.260
this variety of architectures&nbsp;
and short training runs.

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:56.220
So unsurprisingly, VeLOs limitations stand&nbsp;
at long training runs of more than 200,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.220 --> 00:12:03.120
training steps or for large models that have more&nbsp;
than 500 million parameters. On the one hand, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:08.100
fails exactly where hyperparameter search&nbsp;
becomes more costly: in long training runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:13.920
and on large models. But at least it means that&nbsp;
VeLO can help in the most standard applications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:19.500
which are also the ones needed more often in&nbsp;
practice. And that VeLO underperforms in large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:25.020
training regimes is not necessarily the end of the&nbsp;
story but makes us hopeful for the future where

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:31.140
TPU’s won’t start screaming in panic that a single&nbsp;
data point for a network is the entire training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:36.780
run of another network. So, there you have it,&nbsp;
this was Ms. Coffee Bean’s summary of the exciting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:43.320
VeLO paper that proposes to train optimizers from&nbsp;
data rather than hand-tuning hyperparameters.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:46.200
Thanks for watching and see you next time!

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:52.320
Okay, bye!

